WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.960
Hey there! Philip here with InvestaWOW!

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.320
Are you looking to make a GREAT return on
your money, but you’re worried about an

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.350
economic crash?

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:13.769
Why yes I am!

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:18.140
What if I told you that there’s a secret
investment strategy of the rich and famous?

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.460
A safe, secure place to stash your money,
while receiving dividends of 4, 5, even 6%

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.980
per year… even when the market dips!

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.369
That sounds incredible!

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:29.230
But that’s not all.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:33.820
With InvestaWOW, your money grows tax-deferred
from one year to the next.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.620
Buzz off, Uncle Sam!

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.600
You could use all the wealth you build to
plan for retirement.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.860
Or for little Jimmy’s college tuition.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.340
Or a new car!

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.840
In fact, some people are so wild about this
investment, they’ve used it as a replacement

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.800
for banks, lending institutions, insurance,
and more!

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.100
Ooh! I want one! What's it called?

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.500
Whole Life Insurance!

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.780
Huh?

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.360
Stop us if you’ve heard this one.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.160
You’re been thinking about beginning your
investment journey.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.620
You sit down with your local financial adviser,
and they throw you a curve ball:

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.620
Whole Life Insurance as an investment solution.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:23.380
When we asked, 30% of you said you’ve had
a financial expert recommend it.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.220
The rest of you might be thinking, “Life
Insurance?

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.220
As an investment?

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.670
What are you talking about?!”

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.470
Well, as strange as it may appear on its surface,
Whole Life Insurance as an investment has

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.810
been around for generations.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:39.020
It’s life insurance that never expires,
but that’s just the beginning.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.340
It has an investment component too, that’s
protected from the stock market and pays attractive

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.340
dividends.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:51.540
Heck, the first book on investing I ever read
extolled the amazing virtues of Whole Life Insurance.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.800
Let’s start with Life Insurance 101.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:59.880
Way back in 1583 Richard Martin purchased
the first recorded life insurance policy in

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.210
England on his buddy William for 30 pounds.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.319
One year later, when William died, Richard
received a 400 pound payout!

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:10.609
Nice for him, not so nice for Will.

00:02:10.609 --> 00:02:15.540
Today, the principal purpose of life insurance
is to protect those left behind if a family

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.540
member dies.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.700
It’s a standard component of modern financial
planning with 59% of people in the US owning

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.140
some kind of life insurance.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.780
Here’s how it works: you pay a regular “premium”
to insure somebody’s life.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.090
If that person dies while the policy is still
in force, the insurance company pays out a

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:37.459
cash “death benefit”, usually hundreds
of thousands or even millions of dollars.

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:40.420
This money is meant to help those they leave
behind.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.959
The most common type is Term Life.

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:48.159
You have flat, monthly premiums for a set
“Term”; usually 10, 20 or 30 years.

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:52.170
At the end of the term, if you’re still
breathing, premiums jump dramatically, and

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.510
it’s not uncommon to cancel your policy
at that point.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.040
But Whole Life Insurance is different.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.159
It has consistent, flat premiums for your
“whole life”.

00:03:01.159 --> 00:03:03.370
How can they afford to do that?

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.930
By charging a much higher premium.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.689
Part of that goes toward the cost of insurance,
and part of it into an investment account

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:15.819
called the “cash value,” which pays a
dividend of 4-6% per year.

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:21.139
The purpose of this account is to offset the
increasing cost of insurance as you get older

00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:23.180
so your premiums stay flat.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.489
Unlike Term Life, if you cancel a Whole Life
policy, you might get some money back… depending

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:32.099
on how long you’ve had it, you could even
get more than you paid into it!

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:37.169
Some advisers recommend that instead of borrowing
from a bank, you could take out LOANS against

00:03:37.169 --> 00:03:42.939
your cash value while keeping the insurance
in place--a practice dubbed “Infinite Banking”

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:46.870
Advocates of Whole Life treat it a financial
silver-bullet for your needs.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.909
Life Insurance without an expiration date!

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:51.680
An tax-deferred investment for your future!

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.189
Fire your banker and loan money to yourself!

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:56.919
How can Term Life possibly hold a candle to
this?

00:03:56.919 --> 00:03:58.909
I think it’s time to…

00:03:58.909 --> 00:04:00.760
RUN THE NUMBERS!

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.180
Tia and Tamera are twins, both age 40 and
are shopping for life insurance.

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:13.599
Tia picks Whole Life while Tamera picks a
30 Year Term Life.

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:15.920
The death benefit for each is $500,000.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:22.010
Tia will be paying $563 per month for a Whole
Life Policy, according to US averages.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:26.120
That’s pretty steep, but Tia was attracted
to the cash value growth.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:31.090
She’s thinking this will count as some of
her investing each year, and that 5.5% is

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.870
a pretty good return.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.060
But hold on, Tia!

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:41.260
The 5.5% dividend only applies to your cash-value
portion… not the full $563 payment.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:46.200
According to a 2015 Consumer Reports study,
after costs like commissions and fees, the

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:50.400
average Whole Life investment return was closer
to 2%.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.920
If Tia lives to age 70, she’ll have paid
$202,680 in premiums.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.580
But her policy will have a cash value worth almost 280 grand!

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:03.640
She’s been insured her whole life and made
money!

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.870
Sounds like a win to me!

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:09.290
Now Tamera’s 30 Year Term Life is costing
her $52 each month.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:12.750
If she also lives to 70, she gets nothing
back.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:17.910
So Tamera decides to invest the difference
between her premium and what she might have

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.960
paid for a whole life policy.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:24.690
And if she invests in a basic stock index
fund she’ll probably average over 7% for

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:25.870
the next 30 years.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.520
At 70 her life insurance policy will be worthless.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.200
But her investment with be over $600,000!

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.800
That’s twice as much as Tia got with her
Whole Life policy.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.280
So with high premiums and underwhelming returns,
why is Whole Life recommended by brokers and

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.460
advisers across the country?

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.270
One word: Commissions.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:51.420
See, agents and advisers typically receive
a commission of 80-100% of the annual premium.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:56.880
So Tamera’s agent made only $600, but Tia’s
made around 6 grand.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.670
When I first started my financial career,
I too drank the Koolaid.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:04.940
It was clear that our commissions would be
much higher for recommending any insurance

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.150
product with an investment component.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:10.640
Our clients were often unaware of this conflict
of interest.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.270
To be fair, there are some situations that
might make whole-life insurance a good fit.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:19.440
Like a family with a special needs child who
needs a lifetime of support.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.170
Or owning a complicated family business, or
a sizable estate with substantial taxes.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:26.620
But these are special cases.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.280
It’s not the one-size-fits-all remedy it’s
made out to be.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:34.740
It’s best to do your own research, read
the fine print and make sure you’re seeking

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:39.440
objective advice from somebody that doesn’t
have financial skin in the game.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.580
But wait! There's more!

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.100
And that’s our Two Cents!

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.540
If you have any experience with Term or Whole Life Insurance, share it with us in the comments section!

